-- title:
--   Finite generation of the Hecke subalgebra of End J₀(N)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed non-zero. Write $J_0(N)$ for `JZero N`, the group `Pic0 (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (modularFunctionFieldBar N)` of degree-zero divisor classes of the level-$N$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and consider the family `heckeOperatorBar N`, indexed by the primes $\ell$, of $\mathbb Z$-linear endomorphisms of $J_0(N)$: for each prime $\ell$ this is the additive endomorphism `heckeOperatorAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) N ℓ` of the degree-zero Picard group, which is the divisorial Hecke correspondence `heckePic0Bar` built from the data recorded by the predicate `HeckeInputsAlong (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) N ℓ` when that predicate holds and is the zero map otherwise, regarded as a map of $\mathbb Z$-modules. The assertion is that the $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb Z}(J_0(N))$ underlying the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin ℤ (Set.range (heckeOperatorBar N))` generated by all these operators is finitely generated, i.e. satisfies `Submodule.FG`. This is finite generation as a $\mathbb Z$-module only; no freeness, and no statement about the rank, is claimed.
--
--   This is the classical finiteness of the Hecke ring acting on the Jacobian $J_0(N)$: the subring of $\operatorname{End} J_0(N)$ generated by the Hecke operators is module-finite over $\mathbb Z$. It supplies the finiteness input for the torsion and Eisenstein-quotient arguments at level $N$, being cited by [`ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_closure_fg_heckeModuleBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_closure_fg_heckeModuleBar), [`ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_isTorsion_heckeModuleBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_isTorsion_heckeModuleBar) and [`ModularCurve.exists_prime_torsion_of_isMaximal`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_prime_torsion_of_isMaximal).
